From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 20:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 79/79] local: pinctrl: qcom: restore per-interrupt config
 after hibernation

After a cross-boot hibernate resume no TLMM-backed interrupt arrives, so the
keyboard, touchpad, embedded controller and lid switch are all dead. Measured
on x1e80100 by physically typing on the laptop keyboard and moving the
touchpad for ten seconds, twice, on a verified restore (340
platform_pm_restore, 0 platform_pm_thaw):

  irq 200 msmgpio  66 Edge  qcom_x1e_it8987_ec     +0
  irq 211 msmgpio  51 Level hid-over-i2c           +0
  irq 212 msmgpio   3 Level hid-over-i2c           +0
  irq 213 msmgpio  67 Level hid-over-i2c           +0

Zero on every line.

msm_pinctrl_resume() re-applies only the pin mux and config, via
pinctrl_force_default() and the ctl register. The interrupt configuration is
a different register: intr_cfg holds the polarity, the detection type, the
raw-status enable and the target processor, and it is written only by
msm_gpio_irq_set_type() -- which runs when the interrupt is requested and
never again. Across a hibernate the TLMM block comes back from the reboot at
power-on values while irq_desc comes back from the image, so the line ends up
detecting the wrong condition, or routed to a processor that is not us, and
the interrupt never arrives.

This is the same defect as in spmi-pmic-arb, fixed by the companion patch
"spmi: pmic-arb: restore per-interrupt type and polarity after hibernation",
one bus over. There it manifested as a 2400/s interrupt storm because the
mismatch made a level source look edge-triggered; here it manifests as
silence.

Replay the programming for every interrupt the controller has enabled.
irq_set_irq_type() takes the descriptor lock and calls chip->irq_set_type()
unconditionally, so re-applying the type Linux already believes in is enough
to reprogram the hardware and nothing needs saving across the image.
pctrl->enabled_irqs is the same bitmap the chained handler scans, so it is
exactly the set that matters.

.restore_noirq, so it runs before resume_device_irqs() unmasks anything: an
interrupt delivered against a stale configuration is precisely how the PMIC
version of this bug produced its storm.

The before/after intr_cfg values are logged for the first few pins, so one
boot shows whether the register really had been lost rather than leaving that
inferred. On the first run that logging paid for itself twice: it printed
"gpio67 irq cfg restored: 0xe2 -> 0x71", confirming the register really is
lost, and "replayed irq config for 2 gpio(s)" -- far too few, which is how the
iteration bug below was caught.

Iterate every gpio with a mapped irq rather than pctrl->enabled_irqs.
msm_gpio_irq_mask() clears that bitmap and suspend_device_irqs() masks every
non-wakeup interrupt before .restore_noirq runs, so it holds only the few
still unmasked. The masked ones are never fixed up later either, because
resume_device_irqs() ends in msm_gpio_irq_unmask(), which writes the enable
bit and nothing else.

Not board-specific: msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops is shared by every qcom pinctrl
driver, so this covers all of them.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c | 98 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 98 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
@@ -1534,8 +1534,104 @@
 	return pinctrl_force_default(pctrl->pctrl);
 }
 
-SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops, msm_pinctrl_suspend,
-		  msm_pinctrl_resume);
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore: TLMM comes back from the reboot with its per-interrupt
+ * configuration at power-on values, while irq_desc comes back from the image
+ * still describing what was programmed at request time.
+ *
+ * msm_pinctrl_resume() only re-applies the pin mux and config
+ * (pinctrl_force_default(), the ctl register). The interrupt configuration --
+ * polarity, detection type, raw-status enable and, critically, the TARGET
+ * PROCESSOR -- lives in the separate intr_cfg register and is written only by
+ * msm_gpio_irq_set_type(), which runs when the interrupt is requested and
+ * never again. Nothing replays it, so after a cross-boot resume the line is
+ * either detecting the wrong edge/level or routed to a processor that is not
+ * us, and the interrupt simply never arrives.
+ *
+ * Measured on x1e80100 after a cross-boot resume, physically typing on the
+ * laptop keyboard and moving the touchpad for ten seconds, twice:
+ *
+ *   irq 200 msmgpio  66 Edge  qcom_x1e_it8987_ec     +0
+ *   irq 211 msmgpio  51 Level hid-over-i2c           +0
+ *   irq 212 msmgpio   3 Level hid-over-i2c           +0
+ *   irq 213 msmgpio  67 Level hid-over-i2c           +0
+ *
+ * Zero on every line. Keyboard, touchpad, the embedded controller and the lid
+ * switch are all behind TLMM, which is why none of them work after a resume.
+ *
+ * Replay it for every interrupt this controller has enabled.
+ * irq_set_irq_type() takes the descriptor lock and calls chip->irq_set_type()
+ * unconditionally, so re-applying the type Linux already believes in is enough
+ * to reprogram the hardware; nothing has to be saved across the image.
+ * pctrl->enabled_irqs is the same bitmap the chained handler scans, so it is
+ * exactly the set that matters.
+ *
+ * .restore_noirq, so this runs before resume_device_irqs() unmasks anything --
+ * an interrupt delivered against a stale configuration is how the PMIC
+ * equivalent of this bug produced a 2400/s interrupt storm.
+ *
+ * The before/after intr_cfg values are logged once per controller so a single
+ * boot shows whether the register really had been lost.
+ */
+static int __maybe_unused msm_pinctrl_restore_noirq(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct msm_pinctrl *pctrl = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	unsigned int shown = 0;
+	unsigned int count = 0;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	/*
+	 * Every gpio with a mapped irq, NOT just pctrl->enabled_irqs.
+	 * msm_gpio_irq_mask() clears that bitmap and suspend_device_irqs()
+	 * has masked every non-wakeup interrupt before we get here, so by
+	 * restore time it holds only the few still unmasked -- on this
+	 * machine 2 of 5, which is how this was noticed. The masked ones are
+	 * never fixed up afterwards either: resume_device_irqs() ends in
+	 * msm_gpio_irq_unmask(), which writes the enable bit and nothing
+	 * else. irq_find_mapping() plus the trigger-type check below is the
+	 * correct filter.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < pctrl->chip.ngpio; i++) {
+		const struct msm_pingroup *g = &pctrl->soc->groups[i];
+		unsigned int virq, type;
+		struct irq_data *d;
+		u32 before, after;
+
+		virq = irq_find_mapping(pctrl->chip.irq.domain, i);
+		if (!virq)
+			continue;
+
+		d = irq_get_irq_data(virq);
+		if (!d)
+			continue;
+
+		type = irqd_get_trigger_type(d);
+		if (type == IRQ_TYPE_NONE)
+			continue;
+
+		before = msm_readl_intr_cfg(pctrl, g);
+		irq_set_irq_type(virq, type);
+		after = msm_readl_intr_cfg(pctrl, g);
+		count++;
+
+		if (before != after && shown < 4) {
+			dev_info(dev,
+				 "gpio%u irq cfg restored: %#x -> %#x\n",
+				 i, before, after);
+			shown++;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (count)
+		dev_info(dev, "replayed irq config for %u gpio(s)\n", count);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+const struct dev_pm_ops msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops = {
+	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(msm_pinctrl_suspend, msm_pinctrl_resume)
+	.restore_noirq = pm_sleep_ptr(msm_pinctrl_restore_noirq),
+};
 
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops);
 
